const page = {
  title: "Bird Health Screening & Avian Disease Testing Guide | SENO",
  h1: "Bird Health Screening & Biosecurity: A Practical Guide to Avian Disease Testing",
  description: "A practical framework for avian disease screening: choosing tests by species and risk, interpreting PCR results, quarantine and flock biosecurity.",
  canonical: "/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/",
  author: "SENO Avian Genetic Test",
  date: "November 8, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = "<p>Bird health screening is not a single test and it is not a fixed panel that suits every bird. It is a decision process: identify the species and the realistic exposure risk, choose the pathogens worth looking for, select a sample type the laboratory can actually test, and use a method whose limits are understood before the result is acted on.</p>\n<p>That process matters most when birds move. A new parrot, a newly purchased racing pigeon, a group of young birds entering a breeding facility, or a bird returning from a show can change the infection risk of an entire collection. Screening helps a keeper make a better decision, but it does not replace quarantine, veterinary assessment, or daily biosecurity.</p>\n<h2>What Bird Health Screening Means</h2>\n<p>Screening looks for evidence of a pathogen in a bird or a group before clinical signs are obvious. It can be used to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>check a new bird before it meets the existing collection;</li>\n<li>establish a baseline for a breeding group;</li>\n<li>investigate a cluster of illness;</li>\n<li>monitor a loft or aviary over time;</li>\n<li>support a decision about isolation, retesting, or veterinary referral.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screening is different from diagnosis. A laboratory result describes what was found in the sample. A veterinarian interprets that result alongside the bird's history, physical examination, species, age, and the local disease situation.</p>\n<h2>When Birds Should Be Screened</h2>\n<p>The common triggers are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Before introducing a new bird.</strong> The highest-value test is usually the one done before the bird enters the collection, not after a problem appears.</li>\n<li><strong>Before a breeding season.</strong> Breeding pairs, new stock, and young birds may warrant different checks.</li>\n<li><strong>After a show, race, or transport.</strong> Contact with other birds increases exposure risk.</li>\n<li><strong>When a bird is unwell.</strong> Screening may support a veterinary investigation, but it does not replace one.</li>\n<li><strong>During routine monitoring.</strong> A loft or facility may use periodic sampling to detect changes in pathogen circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>After a positive result or an outbreak.</strong> Retesting and tracing help establish whether the finding is isolated or widespread.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There is no universal calendar. Frequency should follow risk, species, housing density, bird movements, and the consequences of introducing an infection.</p>\n<h2>Pathogen Categories and Species Risk</h2>\n<p>A useful screening plan separates pathogens by biology, because a single collection method does not fit all of them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Examples relevant to bird collections</th>\n<th>Why the distinction matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Viruses</td>\n<td>PBFD/BFDV, avian polyomavirus, PPMV-1, pigeon circovirus, pigeon adenovirus</td>\n<td>Some are best detected in feather follicles; others in oral/cloacal swabs; some require RNA testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bacteria</td>\n<td><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>, <em>Salmonella</em> spp., <em>Mycoplasma</em> spp.</td>\n<td>Culture, PCR, and serology answer different questions; zoonotic risk may change the response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protozoa</td>\n<td><em>Trichomonas gallinae</em></td>\n<td>Sample site and transport conditions affect detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fungi</td>\n<td><em>Candida</em> spp., <em>Aspergillus</em> spp.</td>\n<td>A positive result may reflect carriage or secondary disease; clinical context is essential</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Species is the first filter. A test for a pigeon pathogen is not automatically useful for a parrot, and a parrot panel is not a pigeon panel with different labels. The current testing menu, target list, and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory before a batch is planned.</p>\n<h2>Individual Testing vs Flock Screening</h2>\n<p>Individual testing asks what is happening in one bird. Flock screening asks what is happening in a group.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Individual testing</th>\n<th>Flock screening</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main purpose</td>\n<td>Status of a specific bird</td>\n<td>Signal in a group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sampling</td>\n<td>Each bird separately</td>\n<td>Representative samples, sometimes pooled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Result</td>\n<td>Applies to the tested bird</td>\n<td>Applies to the group, with limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best use</td>\n<td>High-value birds, clinical cases, pre-introduction checks</td>\n<td>Large collections, routine monitoring, trend detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Follow-up</td>\n<td>Direct action for that bird</td>\n<td>Individual testing when a pool or group signal is positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Pooling saves laboratory work, but it dilutes individual signal and complicates interpretation. A negative pool does not prove every bird is negative. A positive pool does not identify which bird is positive. The sampling plan, pool size, target prevalence, and assay sensitivity all affect what a group result can mean.</p>\n<h2>Choosing the Right Panel</h2>\n<p>A panel is a tool, not a default. Use this sequence:</p>\n<p><strong>Species → Exposure risk → Target pathogens → Sample type → Testing method.</strong></p>\n<p>For example, a closed parrot collection with no new birds has a different risk profile from a dealer receiving birds from multiple sources. A racing loft before the season has a different risk profile from a breeding loft with young birds. The same laboratory can test both, but the sensible target list is not identical.</p>\n<p>Ask four questions before ordering:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Which pathogens are realistic for this species and this management system?</li>\n<li>What sample type does the assay require?</li>\n<li>Is the question \"is it present?\" or \"is the bird shedding?\" or \"has the bird been exposed?\"</li>\n<li>What will change if the result is positive, negative, or inconclusive?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the answer to the last question is \"nothing\", the test may not be worth taking.</p>\n<h2>PCR, qPCR and Multiplex PCR</h2>\n<p>Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies a specific nucleic-acid target. Real-time PCR (qPCR) monitors amplification as it happens and can provide a cycle-threshold (Ct) value. Multiplex PCR attempts to detect several targets in one reaction.</p>\n<p>These methods are powerful, but they are not mind-reading tools:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A negative result means the target was not detected in that sample at that time. It does not prove the bird is free of infection.</li>\n<li>A positive result means nucleic acid from the target was detected. It does not by itself prove clinical disease.</li>\n<li>Ct values are assay-specific. A Ct from one laboratory or one target cannot be transferred to another as a severity scale.</li>\n<li>Multiplex assays need per-target validation. A panel that detects many targets is not automatically better than a smaller, well-validated panel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Quarantine and Biosecurity</h2>\n<p>Screening is one layer of biosecurity. It does not replace physical separation.</p>\n<p>A workable quarantine plan includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a separate space, preferably with separate airflow and a dedicated entrance;</li>\n<li>dedicated clothing, footwear, and equipment;</li>\n<li>hand hygiene before and after contact;</li>\n<li>separate cleaning materials;</li>\n<li>a written record of arrivals, samples, results, and movements;</li>\n<li>a plan for what happens if a result is positive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Quarantine duration is not one number for all birds. CDC provides at least 30 days as a general separation example for pet birds; VCA discusses 30–45 days for parrot introductions. These are starting points, not universal rules. Species, pathogen, source, housing, and veterinary advice should determine the actual period.</p>\n<h2>Interpreting a Screening Result</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Report term</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What it does not mean</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Detected</td>\n<td>Target nucleic acid was found in the sample</td>\n<td>The bird is necessarily ill or infectious to others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not detected</td>\n<td>Target was not found in that sample</td>\n<td>The bird is guaranteed free of infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inconclusive</td>\n<td>The result cannot be classified confidently</td>\n<td>The bird is positive or negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invalid</td>\n<td>Quality control failed</td>\n<td>The sample has been fully evaluated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A result should be read with the sample type, collection timing, assay target, and the bird's history. When the stakes are high, a second sample or a different method may be more useful than arguing over a single number.</p>\n<h2>How Often to Screen</h2>\n<p>Risk-based monitoring is more defensible than a arbitrary annual schedule.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>New bird: screen before introduction, then follow the quarantine plan.</li>\n<li>Breeding group: consider pre-season baseline testing and targeted checks when birds move.</li>\n<li>Racing loft: increase surveillance before and after the racing season, and after mixing events.</li>\n<li>Facility with an outbreak: test to define the extent, then retest before relaxing controls.</li>\n<li>Closed collection with no movements: less frequent monitoring may be reasonable, but a baseline record is still useful.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The right interval is the one that matches the cost of missing an infection. A high-value breeding collection and a small pet collection do not need the same schedule.</p>\n<h2>Building a Long-Term Flock Health Program</h2>\n<p>A durable program has four parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A bird list.</strong> Individual IDs, species, origin, arrival date, and current group.</li>\n<li><strong>A sample and result log.</strong> Date, sample type, target, laboratory, result, and action.</li>\n<li><strong>A quarantine record.</strong> Start and end dates, observed signs, tests, and veterinary review.</li>\n<li><strong>A review rhythm.</strong> Look at trends, not only individual results; update the plan when birds move or disease pressure changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The goal is not to test everything. It is to make decisions with a documented basis, and to notice change early enough to act.</p>\n<h2>Cluster Guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/bird-health-screening-before-introducing-new-birds/\">Bird Health Screening Before Introducing New Birds</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/avian-disease-testing-for-breeding-facilities/\">Avian Disease Testing for Breeding Facilities</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/pigeon-disease-screening-which-tests-may-be-needed/\">Pigeon Disease Screening: Which Tests May Be Needed?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/parrot-disease-screening-before-purchase-or-breeding/\">Parrot Disease Screening Before Purchase or Breeding</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/bird-quarantine-testing-protocol/\">How to Build a Bird Quarantine Testing Protocol</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/individual-bird-testing-vs-flock-screening/\">Individual Bird Testing vs Flock Screening</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/multiplex-pcr-avian-disease-testing/\">What Is Multiplex PCR in Avian Disease Testing?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/how-often-should-breeding-birds-be-screened/\">How Often Should Breeding Birds Be Screened?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/how-to-interpret-bird-disease-screening-panel/\">How to Interpret a Bird Disease Screening Panel</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/avian-biosecurity-testing-isolation-record-keeping/\">Avian Biosecurity: Testing, Isolation and Record Keeping</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Scenario: A juvenile parrot joins a closed collection</h2>\n<p>A closed collection of six parrots buys an unsexed juvenile Timneh African grey at a weekend bird fair. The seller reports hand-raising but provides no test record. The juvenile can stay in a separate room. The uncertainty is whether exposure occurred at the fair, in the seller's nursery, or not at all.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Record source and contacts, arrange a veterinary examination, and leave introduction open.</li>\n<li>With the veterinarian and laboratory, select species-relevant targets such as PBFD/BFDV, avian polyomavirus, and <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> where the sample and risk support them.</li>\n<li>Submit voluntary screening while the bird remains separated; handle the existing parrots first and use dedicated supplies.</li>\n<li>If Not Detected, continue observation and ask whether the exposure window warrants a retest. Introduction remains a separate decision.</li>\n<li>If Detected or Inconclusive, keep separation, arrange veterinary review, and plan tracing or repeat sampling. Removal is not decided yet.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would screen the juvenile and keep it physically separate until a veterinarian has reviewed the results with its examination, age, and the collection's risks. The reason is to make the sample useful while introduction can still be postponed. This advice would not apply if the bird were ill or covered by a mandatory movement programme; urgent veterinary and official guidance would take priority.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: Three silent returnees join a racing loft</h2>\n<p>A 60-bird racing loft collects three normal-looking birds after a one-loft race. They shared transport and race accommodation with birds from other lofts. The loft can hold the returnees separately and delay basketing. The uncertainty is whether any bird picked up an infection there or was already carrying something.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep the three apart from the racing team, record their crates and contacts, and handle them last.</li>\n<li>Have a veterinarian prioritize PPMV-1, pigeon herpesvirus, <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>, and <em>Salmonella</em>, and confirm sample sites.</li>\n<li>Submit voluntary screening and keep race entries provisional. Do not start treatment because a test is pending.</li>\n<li>If Not Detected, maintain observation and biosecurity, then ask when a repeat sample is warranted. The result does not certify the loft.</li>\n<li>If Detected or Inconclusive, isolate further, trace shared transport, and involve the veterinarian and relevant authorities where reporting applies. Culling is not decided.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would sample the three before they rejoin the team and keep race entries provisional, because a one-loft return is a mixing event. The reason is to support a containment decision while exposure is still recent. This advice would not apply if a bird were visibly ill or a reportable disease were suspected; urgent veterinary and official action would come first.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h3>Combined decision table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Not Detected</td><td>Continue separation and observation; ask whether the exposure window warrants a repeat sample.</td><td>Freedom from infection, absence at every sample site, or safety to introduce or race.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detected in an individual</td><td>Keep that bird separate; the veterinarian reviews clinical findings, contact risk, and follow-up testing.</td><td>That every contact is infected, that the bird is clinically ill, or which treatment is appropriate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detected in a pooled or group sample</td><td>Move to individual or smaller-pool testing and trace shared exposures.</td><td>Which bird carried the target or that the finding is widespread.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inconclusive or Invalid</td><td>Do not release from separation; arrange a new sample or method as advised.</td><td>Presence or absence of the target.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing, including PCR-based screening for selected viral, bacterial, protozoal, and fungal targets. The testing menu and sample requirements change over time; the current target list should be confirmed before a batch is planned. A molecular result is one input into a biosecurity decision. It is not a standalone veterinary diagnosis, and it does not replace physical examination, quarantine, or professional judgement.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Is one negative PCR test enough to clear a new bird?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result means the target was not detected in that sample. Incubation, intermittent shedding, sample site, and assay limits can all produce a negative result in an infected bird. Quarantine and retesting may be appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Should every bird in a collection be tested?</strong></p>\n<p>Not necessarily. Individual testing is useful for high-value or clinically abnormal birds. Group screening can be more practical for large collections, but it answers a group-level question and may require follow-up.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a panel test for every bird disease?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A panel contains a defined list of targets. It cannot detect a pathogen that is not on the list, and it cannot compensate for a poor sample or the wrong sample type.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a positive result mean the bird must be removed?</strong></p>\n<p>That is a veterinary and management decision, not a laboratory decision. The result needs to be interpreted with the bird's condition, the species, the pathogen, and the risk to the rest of the collection.</p>\n<p><strong>How long should a new bird be quarantined?</strong></p>\n<p>There is no single universal duration. CDC describes at least 30 days as a general separation example for pet birds; other sources discuss 30–45 days for parrot introductions. Species, source, pathogen risk, and veterinary advice should shape the plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Can screening replace cleaning and disinfection?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Testing detects what is present in a sample. It does not remove contamination from surfaces, water, equipment, or clothing. Biosecurity depends on isolation, traffic control, and sanitation as well.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>WOAH — Newcastle disease. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Trichomonosis in birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/trichomonosis/trichomonosis-in-birds</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n<li>WOAH — Avian influenza. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/avian-influenza/</li>\n</ol>";
const faqJsonLd = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Is one negative PCR test enough to clear a new bird?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A negative result means the target was not detected in that sample. Incubation, intermittent shedding, sample site, and assay limits can all produce a negative result in an infected bird. Quarantine and retesting may be appropriate."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Should every bird in a collection be tested?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Not necessarily. Individual testing is useful for high-value or clinically abnormal birds. Group screening can be more practical for large collections, but it answers a group-level question and may require follow-up."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can a panel test for every bird disease?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A panel contains a defined list of targets. It cannot detect a pathogen that is not on the list, and it cannot compensate for a poor sample or the wrong sample type."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a positive result mean the bird must be removed?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "That is a veterinary and management decision, not a laboratory decision. The result needs to be interpreted with the bird's condition, the species, the pathogen, and the risk to the rest of the collection."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How long should a new bird be quarantined?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no single universal duration. CDC describes at least 30 days as a general separation example for pet birds; other sources discuss 30–45 days for parrot introductions. Species, source, pathogen risk, and veterinary advice should shape the plan."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can screening replace cleaning and disinfection?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Testing detects what is present in a sam